{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Of all food that may be eaten</b> — Rashi explained this verse according to its midrashic interpretation and derived many things from it: we learned that food is not susceptible [to impurity] unless prepared, etc. And our Rabbis further learned from here that the stillborn of a carcass of impurity does not defile utensils, as we learned in the Mishnah, etc. And we further learned about the entry of water that it does not render [food] susceptible, etc. And among them he said: we further learned that food does not defile others unless it contains the volume of an egg, as it is stated, \"that may be eaten\" — food that is eaten in one swallow, and the Sages measured that the gullet can hold no more than a hen's egg. This is the language of the Rav.\n\nAnd there is compression in it, for this verse concerns the impurity of the foods themselves, and it does not speak of their defiling others. But the Rav derived this from what they said in Torat Kohanim (chapter 9, section 1): \"Food shall become impure\" — this teaches that it becomes impurity in any quantity. One might think it defiles others in any quantity? Scripture teaches: \"that may be eaten\" — thus it does not defile unless [it is the size of] an egg.\n\nAnd they already disputed with the Rav and brought proofs that foods do not receive impurity from the Torah at all unless [they are the size of] an egg, and the midrash in Torat Kohanim is an asmakhta of their words — that they themselves added that [foods] receive impurity in any quantity. And the essential meaning of the verse is to be expounded as they said there: \"The food\" — to exclude animal foods; \"of all food\" — to include animal foods that one intended for human consumption; \"that may be eaten\" — excluding putrid food."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The Rav refers to Maimonides; here Ramban cites and critiques his position on the minimum quantity for food impurity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:03.497068139Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}